---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/justuno';
const title = 'Justuno for Shopify: qualify the capture handoff';
const desc = 'Evaluate Justuno availability, storefront capture, Postscript handoff, segmentation experiments and operating costs.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Justuno for Shopify: qualify the capture handoff</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Justuno is a candidate for improving what a shopper shares on the storefront and how that information reaches a messaging tool. The useful question is whether a relevant capture experience produces usable, permissioned records. Evaluate that handoff before paying for a broader experiment program; no merchant lift or supplier rank is established here.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Availability comes before capture design</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/justuno-social-email-leads">Shopify listing reached from the Justuno app URL</a> currently says the app is unavailable. That is a constraint on new installation through this listing, not proof that every existing connection has stopped. Ask Justuno to confirm the supported installation route for this store before planning a new launch.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://hub.justuno.com/article/511-install-justuno-shopify">installation guide</a> describes storefront embed, cart and conversion tracking, product/order/customer ingestion, a theme embed switch and manual code for headless stores. It also says Justuno experiences do not appear on checkout pages following the checkout.liquid change. Existing users should verify their live theme and tracking rather than infer readiness from an old install receipt.</p>
      <p>Capture forms, targeted offers and product recommendations can serve distinct jobs. A collection-specific prompt may make a signup more relevant; a recommendation may help a shopper without collecting a contact. Keep those benefits separate when measuring results. Neither requires a compulsory Sequenzy pairing, and a contact captured onsite is not itself a lifecycle send.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Prove one destination before expanding the quiz</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://hub.justuno.com/article/470-syncing-postscript-to-justuno">Postscript integration instructions</a> require suitable API access, authentication and a Sync to App action in a workflow. This gives a concrete SMS handoff to evaluate. It does not establish that every quiz property becomes a Shopify tag or that an arbitrary sender has a native connector.</p>
      <p>Prepare a field contract: normalized phone or email, capture source, selected interest, form version, permission evidence and destination field. Mark which fields travel directly to the sender and which travel through Shopify or a separately maintained connector. For a skincare quiz, skin interest can guide content, but should not be treated as a medical diagnosis or SMS permission.</p>
      <p>Use a fresh subscriber, existing email-only contact, already subscribed phone, withdrawn contact and corrected phone number. Inspect destination records and activity logs for each. An accepted connection is different from a successful record update; a record update is different from eligibility to enter a send flow. Repeat a submission to check duplicate entries and simulate a failed sync followed by retry.</p>
      <p>The storefront owner maintains prompts and field names; the destination owner maintains eligibility, suppression and welcome steps. Record failures for repair without replaying a welcome twice. A purchase arriving after capture should cancel an obsolete acquisition incentive where appropriate. Do not rely on an interest tag as a permanent copy of current order or subscription state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed storefront exercises</h2>
      <p>These are acceptance and experiment designs, not observed merchant tests. Example offers and timing rules are inputs to choose for the store. Validate that each required experience and field is available in the account before promising it to shoppers.</p>
      <p><strong>Skincare quiz:</strong> propose four questions with dry, oily, sensitive and combination interests and an optional 15% welcome offer. First verify answer mapping and separate channel choices. Then compare relevant welcome content with generic content among eligible signups, keeping assignment stable. Quiz completion, successful handoff, purchase and withdrawal each need their own denominator; do not repeat the unsupported 24% click-lift claim.</p>
      <p><strong>Home-goods exit offer:</strong> propose a $120 cart qualification and seven-day repeat-display suppression. Test a changing cart, empty cart, returning purchaser and a mobile visitor without desktop exit behavior. Compare the qualified rule with another capture policy in randomized eligible traffic. Check contribution after free shipping, not just new addresses collected.</p>
      <p><strong>Denim landing page:</strong> use collection-specific creative for a paid social landing page. Preserve collection and source separately; a UTM is acquisition context, not a lasting product preference. Test stripped query strings, direct return visits and shoppers who later buy another collection. Evaluate downstream relevance without silently overwriting a shopper’s explicit answer.</p>
      <p><strong>Supplement recommendations:</strong> propose complementary SKUs on a supported storefront surface. Validate stock, variant, price and destination before enabling the module. Randomize eligible sessions and measure contribution per assigned session, including no-purchase sessions. AOV among purchasers alone can rise while total contribution falls; there is no demonstrated $11.40 uplift here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Budget capture volume and the downstream workload</h2>
      <p>Get a dated quote for the supported account and installation route, including the usage unit, allowances, excess traffic, recommendation features and support. The old $49–79 and $150–220 examples are not verified prices. Confirm whether viral or bot traffic counts, how caps behave and whether an experiment pauses when an allowance is exhausted.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical 80,000 eligible sessions, signup rates of 2.8% and 4.2% imply 2,240 and 3,360 signups: a difference of 1,120. These are arithmetic inputs, not a Privy baseline or Justuno result. If all additional contacts qualify for three welcome emails, that adds up to 3,360 email attempts before purchases, withdrawals and delivery exclusions. SMS requires its own eligibility and credit calculation.</p>
      <p>Include sender charges, discount cost, connector work, creative production and investigation of sync failures. Cost per usable signup divides total capture cost by new contacts that reach the intended destination with usable evidence, rather than every form submission. For an onsite recommendation, use incremental contribution and module maintenance instead of allocating fictitious email revenue.</p>
      <p>Low traffic does not create an automatic 25,000-session downgrade rule. Choose an effect size and feasible experiment duration, account for repeat visitors and report uncertainty. If the store cannot maintain the field contract or enough shoppers see the experience, a simpler existing form may be the better operational choice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Keep capture ownership distinct from sending ownership</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/apps/mailchimp">Mailchimp review</a> follows a synced audience and order-aware automations. This page follows a storefront submission into a separate destination. The <a class="link" href="/apps/firepush">Firepush review</a> follows browser subscriptions, which cannot be assumed to match a captured phone or email. Compare identity models before comparing conversion dashboards.</p>
      <p>For a capture alternative such as <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy</a>, require the same destination fields, eligible traffic, display constraints and cost workload. No claim that Privy is always cheaper or less capable follows from this review. Retain Justuno where the supported route, relevant experiences and reliable handoff justify the additional maintenance.</p>
      <p>Before changing capture tools, preserve source and permission history and stop obsolete forms. Keep the current sender responsible for opt-outs and queued messages until the replacement path is verified. Review failed syncs, duplicate submissions and welcome entry counts alongside signup totals. There is no universal twenty-four-hour channel rule or order-volume trigger for adding another supplier.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
